DC's Titans has earned a mostly positive  review from fans and critics but DC  universes flagship series is far from  perfect these are our picks for the 5  best and 5 worst things about Titan so  far Titan season 1 introduced villains  the nuclear family who quickly  established themselves as being funny  creepy and absolutely vicious the  nuclear family of the comics were  somewhat different they had a different  origin different powers and never  actually locked horns with the Titans  the first appearance was 1985 s The  Outsiders number 1 where they were  actually all robots  despite their safe suburban appearance  these villains are truly brutal on the  show going so far as putting dub in a  lengthy coma through matically their  perfect adversaries for the Titans just  about all the Titans are either orphans  Runaways or both while the sadistic  nuclear czar a twisted parody of what  society would have us believe is a true  family sorry kids Ryan Potter does a  good job as gar Logan aka Beast Boy but  fans of the character have still been  frustrated by how limited his powers  have seemed so far in the comics gar can  turn into just about any kind of animal  it doesn't matter if the animal is  native to earth or even if it's ever  existed yet for all of titans first  season gar only uses his powers to  change into a tiger Tigers have always  been my favorite ever since I was a kid  the show is a justification for gars  limited skill set but it still takes  away from the fun of wondering what  Beast Boy will change into next the  situation is starting to get better  though in the season 2 premiere shortly  after the Trigon possessed Titans beat  gar to a pulp we see him change into a  snake hopefully we'll see some more  variety in the episodes to come current  Walter's first appeared as Jason Todd  aka the new Robin halfway through Titans  first season and it wasn't clear if the  young braggart would be a regular  feature but Jason shows up in the season  2 premiere and by the end of the episode  he's joined the team at Titans Tower in  San Francisco you're the new Robin  present and accounted for babe hey Jason  adds an unstable element to the Titans  he's hot blooded he doesn't respect  Authority and doesn't play well with  others  and comics readers know that he's a  ticking time bomb Jason was famously  killed by the Joker in the classic death  in the family storyline returning years  later as the lethal vigilante red  hood Titans is pretty light on action  and when a fight scene does kick off  it's often a little underwhelming Titans  is good at more grounded action like  scenes have Dick Grayson taking on a  bunch of thugs in an alleyway but the  team now includes a girl with demonic  powers a boy who can change into deadly  beasts a fighter comparable to Wonder  Woman and a super-strong alien who can  hurl energy blasts at her folks so why  are so many fights just simple  hand-to-hand combat it's rare to see  even more than two or three of the  heroes fighting together granted Titans  is still just a TV show and you can't  expect big-budget special effects but  what's the point of a superhero team-up  series if we don't get to see them all  teaming up for a giant fight despite  their silly costumes the story of Hawk  and Dove is proven to be one of titans  most compelling arcs some of the best  episodes of the first season focused on  their different motives for becoming  crime fighter's Hawks struggle with drug  addiction and doves brief romance with  Dick Grayson hopefully we'll see more of  the pair in the second season and beyond  DC's most powerful superheroes exist in  the universe of titans but it doesn't  always feel like it all the big names  are sidelined and the longer the show  goes on the more conspicuous their  absences feel it's a big house it's  funny that I only ever see two of you  it's almost like the studio couldn't  afford another x-men we've already seen  the show's creative team struggling to  explain the absence of characters from  the expanded universe and not just the  ones in capes in the season 2 premiere  when Hawk and Dove go to the Wayne  mansion looking for Batman Jason Todd  has to break some bad news  Bruce is halfway across the world with  the rest of the Justice League Alfred to  wait Alfred - why would Alfred need to  go on assignment with the Justice League  they can't make their own tea it may  have a frustrating cliffhanger but the  season 1 finale was by far one of titans  best episodes  Trigon subjects dick to a nightmare  vision of his old mentor finally  snapping and going on a rampage  throughout Gotham City with dick being  the only one who could stop him which he  does permanently the decision to give in  to his most violent urges puts dick  under trigons control dicks adventure in  the illusory Gotham is an example of how  Titans uses the expanded universe to its  advantage we only see glimpses of  characters like Batman Joker and  two-face but in this episode at least  it's all we need to see the only a list  superhero we've actually seen on Titans  is Bruce Wayne who shows up for the  first time in the sea  and to premier you'd be hard-pressed to  challenge Ian Glen's talent especially  after his memorable turn his draw more  mods on Game of Thrones  but it's questionable whether or not  he's right for this particular role for  one his American accent isn't the most  convincing thing in the world and at 58  years old he also seems just a little  bit too old to be playing The Dark  Knight Glen isn't too old to play a  retired Batman but one who's still  active with the Justice League that's a  stretch in the comics Dick Grayson  eventually comes out from Batman's  shadow sets his Robin costume aside and  rebrands himself as the hero Nightwing  as Nightwing dick has a darker less  Peter Pan s costume and he eventually  leaves Gotham to find his own stomping  grounds in the fictional city of  Bludhaven Titans has been teasing this  development since the first season and  it seems like just a matter of time  before dick adopts the new duds toward  the end of 2018  Titans showrunner Greg Walker confirmed  that the season 2 premiere episode  Trigon was originally meant to be the  season 1 finale he claimed that this was  changed because he loved the idea of  ending the first season with a  cliffhanger and starting the next with a  bang but after watching the episode you  might wonder if the actual reason was  because the culmination of the story  just isn't satisfying in Trigon almost  every hero besides Rachel and gar do  nothing to contribute to the main  villains defeat most of the heroes get  possessed then just stand around looking  possessed  Rachel insists she has to predict from  possession before fighting Trigon but  it's not like she really needs his help  how does she defeat the all-powerful  demon we've been told can destroy the  universe she walks up to him says  something snarky unleashes her power and  just kind of vaguely wins how she beats  him isn't really spelled out at all so  much for that storyline check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  shows are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

